.TH CHMAP_REDECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_redeclare \- re-establish the companion type variables for a hash map handle from another scope
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_redeclare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares the companion type variables that carry key_t and val_t for a
hash map handle that already exists. It declares no handle and neither
creates nor destroys a map; the only thing it declares again is the type
information that the type-inferred macros need.

Every type-inferred macro learns the key and value types of a map from
two ordinary local variables named after the handle, which
chmap_construct(3) and chmap_declare(3) create next to it. Since they are
local declarations, they don't travel with the handle into another
function. In that function, chmap_insert(hm_name, ...) and the other
macros refer to variables that are not declared there, and the
translation unit doesn't compile. This macro declares them again, so any
code that passes a chmap across a function boundary needs it.

The types given here must be the ones the map was created with. The
macros use them to read a key, to size a value and to decide whether a
value is a char * that the map stores by contents.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name of the existing map handle
key_t    Key type the map was created with
val_t    Value type the map was created with
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations and is not an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Expands to a sequence of declarations, so put it where a declaration is
allowed; it takes a trailing semicolon of its own
The declared variables are pointers that nothing dereferences at run
time. They are only there so that typeof and the type-introspection
macros can read the types from them, and they cost nothing in the
generated code
Since it only declares variables, it is also how you use a handle that
lives in a struct field or in a global: name it in each function that
works on that handle
An array key or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
a key or a value by assignment and C cannot assign an array, so the map
would store the address of the caller's array instead of its elements. A
struct that holds the array works
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not declare a value type whose width differs from the stored one.
chmap_get(3) and chmap_get_ptr(3) catch such a width at run time, describe
the size mismatch and stop the program through _ccol_fatal_err(). A
mismatch between two types of the same width goes unnoticed, so the
types here are a promise that you keep yourself and the compiler does
not check.
A key of the wrong width is reported instead of accepted, and the raw
entry points return ccol_invalid_args for it, because the hash of a key
and its equality test mean something only at the width the map was
created for. chmap_get(3) and chmap_insert(3) turn that error into
_ccol_fatal_err(). chmap_get_ptr(3) and chmap_remove(3) report it
without stopping: chmap_get_ptr(3) gives a NULL result and
chmap_remove(3) gives the return code.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void bump(chmap scores, const char *who) {
    chmap_redeclare(scores, char *, int);
    int *slot = chmap_get_ptr(scores, who);
    if (slot) {
        (*slot)++;
    }
}

chmap_construct(scores, char *, int);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
bump(scores, "alice");
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR chmap_insert (3)
